\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{When a marginal precision is a restriction}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{Q>0} be self-adjoint on a finite-dimensional real Euclidean
space #{H} and let #{P} be an orthogonal projector. Assume its image
#{X} and kernel #{Y} are nonzero. Write #{\iota_X:X\to H} and
#{\iota_Y:Y\to H} for the inclusions, and define
##{A=\iota_X^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_X,\qquad K=\iota_X^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_Y,\qquad D=\iota_Y^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_Y.}
Then #{D>0}. The [selected precision](fdip-005B) obeys
##{S_P=A-KD^{-1}K^{\dagger}\preceq A.}
Here #{T\preceq A} means that #{\langle x,(A-T)x\rangle\ge0} for
every #{x\in X}. The following conditions are equivalent:
##{\begin{aligned}
S_P=A&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad K=0\\
&\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad QX\subseteq X
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad PQ=QP.
\end{aligned}}
In this case #{A} is the restriction of #{Q} to the invariant
subspace #{X}; call the selector \newvocab{restricting}.
Otherwise it \newvocab{induces a different precision} through
elimination of the complement. Orthonormal block matrices and the
matrix positivity chart are given in \ref{fdip-00LN}.}

\proof{
\p{The Schur formula is proved in \ref{fdip-005B}. Its difference
from the compression satisfies
##{\langle x,(A-S_P)x\rangle
 =\|D^{-1/2}K^{\dagger}x\|^2\ge0.}
It vanishes for all #{x} exactly when #{K^{\dagger}=0}, since
#{D^{-1/2}} is invertible. The #{Y}-component of #{Q(\iota_Xx)} is
#{K^{\dagger}x}, so the same condition is #{QX\subseteq X}.
Self-adjointness then makes #{Y=X^\perp} invariant as well:
for #{y\in Y} and #{x\in X},
#{\langle Qy,x\rangle=\langle y,Qx\rangle=0}.
Thus #{Q} preserves both summands, so it commutes with #{P}.
Conversely #{PQ=QP} carries the image of #{P} to itself.}
}

\p{When #{K\ne0}, the positive semidefinite difference
#{A-S_P} is nonzero, so the quadratic form decreases in some
direction. It need not be positive definite: some directions may
have zero loss. The assertion is a Loewner inequality with the
stated equality criterion, not a strict inequality in every direction.}

\p{For a positive-weight graph and a coordinate selector #{P_R},
let #{\delta_v} be the counting basis vector at a vertex #{v}.
If #{v} is retained and #{u} is eliminated, the pairing
#{\langle\delta_v,Q\delta_u\rangle} equals minus the sum of the
weights of the edges whose endpoints are exactly #{v} and #{u}.
Orientation does not affect the sum, and a loop does not contribute.
All summands are positive. Hence #{K=0} exactly when no edge joins
#{R} to its complement. In a connected graph every nonempty proper
coordinate selector therefore induces a different precision. In
particular, the retained vertices in [cycle decimation](fdip-0057)
are stable under the retained cyclic action, but this symmetry does
not make their span #{Q}-invariant.}
